1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,800
It's the seeds of civilization and the sustenance

2
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:07,300
of humankind.

3
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:11,340
One acre will feed 9,000 people for one day.

4
00:00:11,440 --> 00:00:14,540
Harvested by an elaborate convoy of man and machine.

5
00:00:14,640 --> 00:00:17,660
What girl wouldn't like to drive a huge convoy?

6
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:20,260
So mighty, it feeds millions.

7
00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:23,760
Yet so vulnerable, it depends on modern science.

8
00:00:23,860 --> 00:00:28,960
A disease could devastate a crop in a matter of 10 days.

9
00:00:28,961 --> 00:00:32,960
From bread to beer, pasta to plastic,

10
00:00:33,060 --> 00:00:35,560
it's a spectacular journey from the field

11
00:00:35,660 --> 00:00:37,160
to your dinner table.

12
00:00:37,260 --> 00:00:40,900
Now, the untold story of wheat on Modern Marvels.

13
00:00:52,720 --> 00:00:55,120
Beyond the amber waves of grain,

14
00:00:55,220 --> 00:00:58,960
over a million dollars' worth of equipment sits idle.

15
00:00:58,961 --> 00:01:03,200
These are the machines of the Meisner family, one of 700

16
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:07,160
crews known as custom harvesters who haul in nearly half of the

17
00:01:07,260 --> 00:01:09,700
United States wheat crop.

18
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:12,600
Each year, they race against time to gather the delicate

19
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:13,700
grain.

20
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:17,320
Once the crop is ready, you need to be right on top of it,

21
00:01:17,420 --> 00:01:19,020
all within a week's time.

22
00:01:19,120 --> 00:01:22,920
And we actually follow the harvest of the wheat as it

23
00:01:23,020 --> 00:01:25,320
matures north.

24
00:01:25,420 --> 00:01:27,560
The responsibility of feeding the world

25
00:01:27,561 --> 00:01:30,200
the world weighs heavily on their shoulders.

26
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:32,560
Any holdup at harvest could be catastrophic.

27
00:01:35,540 --> 00:01:37,800
At their stop in Elk City, Oklahoma,

28
00:01:37,900 --> 00:01:41,700
the Meissner crew has been delayed by a recent rainstorm.

29
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:45,340
And this custom harvester is desperate to get rolling again.

30
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:47,420
When these machines of mine are not running,

31
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:50,720
it's costing me $1,500 an hour.

32
00:01:50,820 --> 00:01:52,860
We've been sitting for a couple of days here,

33
00:01:52,960 --> 00:01:55,160
but we've had a good strong wind here.

34
00:01:55,161 --> 00:01:58,000
so hopefully this wheat is gonna run now.

35
00:01:58,100 --> 00:02:01,960
But we'll just get out here and see what we got for a sample

36
00:02:02,060 --> 00:02:04,700
and check things out.

37
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:07,800
Before harvest can begin, the moisture level of the wheat

38
00:02:07,900 --> 00:02:13,520
must be under 13.5%, any higher, and the wheat could spoil.

39
00:02:13,620 --> 00:02:16,680
The head, or spike of the wheat, contains the kernels,

40
00:02:16,780 --> 00:02:19,120
or grain, which are the only part of the plant

41
00:02:19,220 --> 00:02:20,820
used for human food.

42
00:02:20,920 --> 00:02:23,020
We're gonna grab a few heads here,

43
00:02:23,021 --> 00:02:27,020
And the object is we're gonna put them in my palm here,

44
00:02:27,021 --> 00:02:30,020
and we're gonna thrash those kernels out of that head

45
00:02:30,021 --> 00:02:33,020
and then bite on them.

46
00:02:33,021 --> 00:02:36,020
And you can hear them crack, pop.

47
00:02:36,021 --> 00:02:39,020
It's gonna be ready to go.

48
00:02:39,021 --> 00:02:43,020
We got a couple of soft ones, but it really tastes pretty darn good.

49
00:02:43,021 --> 00:02:45,020
We're gonna go. We're gonna try it.

50
00:02:45,021 --> 00:02:47,020
My crew's anxious to start cutting wheat.

51
00:02:47,021 --> 00:02:50,020
But we're gonna get going here.

52
00:02:50,021 --> 00:02:52,420
OK, Emma, get them organized.

53
00:02:52,520 --> 00:02:53,420
Break them up.

54
00:02:53,520 --> 00:02:54,420
We're going to cut weight.

55
00:02:57,420 --> 00:02:58,920
Copy the combines.

56
00:02:59,020 --> 00:03:01,560
Go ahead and raise all your heads up, pull in,

57
00:03:01,660 --> 00:03:05,360
and just make a nice gradual sweep.

58
00:03:05,460 --> 00:03:07,680
The vast majority of the crop they harvest

59
00:03:07,780 --> 00:03:10,680
is hard red winter wheat, which ultimately becomes

60
00:03:10,780 --> 00:03:13,720
the staff of life, bread.

61
00:03:13,820 --> 00:03:17,420
Harvest starts in Texas every May and wraps up in Canada

62
00:03:17,520 --> 00:03:19,320
in September.

63
00:03:19,321 --> 00:03:22,220
By that time, the Meisner team alone

64
00:03:22,320 --> 00:03:24,700
will harvest more than 1 million bushels,

65
00:03:24,800 --> 00:03:29,400
enough wheat to feed nearly half a million people for a year.

66
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:32,640
That's if the weather doesn't best them.

67
00:03:32,740 --> 00:03:34,340
We've been in hail.

68
00:03:34,440 --> 00:03:36,540
We have been in the field with a tornado

69
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:38,040
within a quarter of a mile of us.

70
00:03:38,140 --> 00:03:40,980
We do a lot of praying to the good Lord.

71
00:03:41,080 --> 00:03:43,540
And their machines don't break down.

72
00:03:43,640 --> 00:03:45,240
It's a family operation.

73
00:03:45,340 --> 00:03:47,440
My wife and I started with, had two combines.

74
00:03:47,540 --> 00:03:48,440
She drove one.

75
00:03:48,441 --> 00:03:50,240
She drove one, I drove one.

76
00:03:50,340 --> 00:03:52,540
We have six children, four daughters and two sons.

77
00:03:52,640 --> 00:03:56,640
Every one of the kids have grown up on the floor of a combine.

78
00:03:56,740 --> 00:03:59,880
Now their operation has grown to five combines.

79
00:03:59,980 --> 00:04:02,380
It's equipment and manpower that many wheat farmers

80
00:04:02,480 --> 00:04:04,180
can't afford.

81
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:07,200
They can actually get our wheat out in three days,

82
00:04:07,300 --> 00:04:10,700
whereas it would take us almost two weeks with one machine.

83
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:13,840
Being a wheat farmer probably is being one of the biggest

84
00:04:13,940 --> 00:04:15,540
gamblers there is.

85
00:04:15,640 --> 00:04:17,900
You've got a crop worth hundreds of thousands.

86
00:04:17,901 --> 00:04:23,200
in the field that can be wiped out in the matter of 30 minutes.

87
00:04:23,300 --> 00:04:25,500
These gamblers depend on the custom harvester

88
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:28,840
to be their ace in the hole.

89
00:04:28,940 --> 00:04:30,740
Though custom harvesting with combines

90
00:04:30,840 --> 00:04:33,280
has been around for more than 100 years,

91
00:04:33,380 --> 00:04:36,760
the industry really took hold in the 1940s.

92
00:04:36,860 --> 00:04:40,220
But some crews became hasty and dishonest.

93
00:04:40,320 --> 00:04:43,160
Farmers tagged them harvest gypsies.

94
00:04:43,260 --> 00:04:46,020
This perception stuck until the 1960s,

95
00:04:46,021 --> 00:04:50,220
became a reputable profession.

96
00:04:50,320 --> 00:04:52,660
Today's custom cutters are the dependable warriors

97
00:04:52,760 --> 00:04:55,260
of the wheat harvest.

98
00:04:55,360 --> 00:04:59,560
They can clean out 100 acres per combine per day.

99
00:04:59,660 --> 00:05:02,140
Emma Meisner has been behind the wheel of a combine

100
00:05:02,240 --> 00:05:04,580
since she was 10 years old.

101
00:05:04,680 --> 00:05:07,680
At first, when you get into it, you know,

102
00:05:07,780 --> 00:05:10,580
having driven before, it's really nerve wracking.

103
00:05:10,680 --> 00:05:13,620
There's a lot of things to watch at one time,

104
00:05:13,621 --> 00:05:15,660
But it's just fun.

105
00:05:15,760 --> 00:05:17,960
It's just like mowing the lawn with a 30-foot head.

106
00:05:18,060 --> 00:05:22,700
You know, what girl wouldn't like to drive a huge combine?

107
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:25,860
As each 30-foot wide, 30,000-pound combine

108
00:05:25,960 --> 00:05:28,460
rolls through a field, a spinning reel

109
00:05:28,560 --> 00:05:31,140
pulls the weed into a razor-sharp sickle bar

110
00:05:31,240 --> 00:05:33,640
that cuts the stalks.

111
00:05:33,740 --> 00:05:35,380
This is very, very sharp.

112
00:05:35,480 --> 00:05:39,640
It will cut anything off that comes in front of it.

113
00:05:39,740 --> 00:05:42,180
So this is very, very hardened material.

114
00:05:42,181 --> 00:05:46,820
It's a special alloy steel that is heat treated.

115
00:05:46,920 --> 00:05:49,220
These knives are not sharpened.

116
00:05:49,320 --> 00:05:52,820
They stay sharp for the duration of the life.

117
00:05:52,920 --> 00:05:55,120
Once cut, an auger moves the stalks

118
00:05:55,220 --> 00:05:58,760
through the feeder house and into the heart of the machine,

119
00:05:58,860 --> 00:06:01,540
where the grain is separated from the stem.

120
00:06:01,640 --> 00:06:03,460
The huge force of a cylinder spinning

121
00:06:03,560 --> 00:06:07,100
against a concave at up to 960 RPM

122
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:09,740
knocks the grain off the stem.

123
00:06:09,741 --> 00:06:12,440
This process is called threshing.

124
00:06:12,540 --> 00:06:14,680
The separated grain then falls through the grates

125
00:06:14,780 --> 00:06:16,840
of the concave into a cleaning system

126
00:06:16,940 --> 00:06:20,880
before it's moved into the holding tank.

127
00:06:20,980 --> 00:06:24,580
Since each tank can only hold about 300 bushels of wheat,

128
00:06:24,680 --> 00:06:30,200
the combine must unload on the move nearly every 30 minutes.

129
00:06:30,300 --> 00:06:33,660
I go from combine to combine and take their wheat

130
00:06:33,760 --> 00:06:38,060
and put it in the grain cart and then bring it to the truck

131
00:06:38,061 --> 00:06:42,060
So the truck can bring the wheat to market.

132
00:06:42,160 --> 00:06:44,400
The semis make multiple trips back and forth

133
00:06:44,500 --> 00:06:47,900
to the grain elevator each day.

134
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:50,400
A holdup with the trucks could mean serious setbacks

135
00:06:50,500 --> 00:06:52,800
in the field.

136
00:06:52,900 --> 00:06:55,420
Sometimes there will be no line of trucks here.

137
00:06:55,520 --> 00:06:57,620
You can just pull up on the scale.

138
00:06:57,720 --> 00:07:00,720
Sometimes it'll be like this.

139
00:07:00,820 --> 00:07:06,220
I could say we could be here at least a good hour.

140
00:07:06,221 --> 00:07:10,360
Luckily, we've got a couple other trucks back at the field.

141
00:07:10,460 --> 00:07:12,720
This is no 9-to-5 job.

142
00:07:12,820 --> 00:07:15,560
We can run 24-7 if we needed to.

143
00:07:15,660 --> 00:07:18,500
And I have run all night long, knowing that if there

144
00:07:18,600 --> 00:07:20,800
would be a rainstorm coming out in the morning,

145
00:07:20,900 --> 00:07:23,440
we would cut right till we would get rained on.

146
00:07:23,540 --> 00:07:27,400
We stay generally on the farmer's place.

147
00:07:27,500 --> 00:07:29,080
We have three travel trailers.

148
00:07:29,180 --> 00:07:32,340
We have a main house where all the meals are served out of.

149
00:07:32,440 --> 00:07:33,340
In line.

150
00:07:33,440 --> 00:07:35,480
My wife is a fantastic cook.

151
00:07:35,481 --> 00:07:38,480
She can cook anything and everything on a grill.

152
00:07:38,481 --> 00:07:39,480
You name it, she can do it all.

153
00:07:41,480 --> 00:07:43,480
And then we have a crew trailer,

154
00:07:43,481 --> 00:07:45,480
which all the guys are in.

155
00:07:45,481 --> 00:07:47,480
This is our home away from home.

156
00:07:47,481 --> 00:07:48,480
It's kind of small.

157
00:07:48,481 --> 00:07:51,480
We got anywhere from three to seven guys living in here,

158
00:07:51,481 --> 00:07:54,480
kind of depending on the harvest year.

159
00:07:54,481 --> 00:07:56,480
Back here we have three bunks.

160
00:07:56,481 --> 00:07:59,480
They kind of stack them in here, kind of tight.

161
00:07:59,481 --> 00:08:02,480
The top two each have a window.

162
00:08:02,481 --> 00:08:04,480
The bottom one does not.

163
00:08:04,481 --> 00:08:06,880
We like to refer to that as the burrow.

164
00:08:06,881 --> 00:08:10,180
And then this is where the other three guys sleep.

165
00:08:10,181 --> 00:08:13,020
This bed down here is actually where I sleep.

166
00:08:13,021 --> 00:08:15,420
Not a small guy, so the easiest way to get in the bed

167
00:08:15,421 --> 00:08:17,320
is kind of lay on the floor and you just kind of roll in.

168
00:08:17,321 --> 00:08:18,620
And then when you get up in the morning,

169
00:08:18,621 --> 00:08:19,920
you just kind of roll out.

170
00:08:19,921 --> 00:08:21,640
It's pretty comfortable, I mean, by the time you get done

171
00:08:21,641 --> 00:08:24,140
working 12, 14-hour days, whatever it is,

172
00:08:24,141 --> 00:08:27,740
you're so beat, you just want to go to sleep.

173
00:08:27,741 --> 00:08:32,580
The work is very, very hard, but it's so gratifying

174
00:08:32,581 --> 00:08:37,180
because people entrust you with such large responsibility.

175
00:08:37,280 --> 00:08:39,280
The job we have just completed, we

176
00:08:39,380 --> 00:08:43,720
did well over 3,000 acres in just over a week's time.

177
00:08:43,820 --> 00:08:49,940
We almost held off a million dollars' worth of wheat.

178
00:08:50,040 --> 00:08:51,940
But there's no time to rest.

179
00:08:52,040 --> 00:08:54,240
The Meisners must roll onto the next wheat farm

180
00:08:54,340 --> 00:08:57,840
before Mother Nature strikes again.

181
00:08:57,940 --> 00:09:01,740
They still have 400 fields in three more states to harvest

182
00:09:01,741 --> 00:09:04,780
before their work ends.

183
00:09:04,880 --> 00:09:07,040
The Combine Caravan has become commonplace

184
00:09:07,140 --> 00:09:10,980
in the Great Plains.

185
00:09:11,080 --> 00:09:14,880
But just 200 years ago, farmers could only rely on manpower

186
00:09:14,980 --> 00:09:17,780
to harvest their crop.

187
00:09:17,880 --> 00:09:20,200
Cutting wheat was done solely by hand,

188
00:09:20,300 --> 00:09:23,900
with a long, sharp cutting blade called a scythe.

189
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:27,940
Back in those days, the fields were nothing compared

190
00:09:28,040 --> 00:09:30,300
with the size of these fields.

191
00:09:30,301 --> 00:09:33,740
You'd have a heart attack before you got a field like this cut.

192
00:09:34,540 --> 00:09:38,060
It was a little slow, but it was good, honest, hard work.

193
00:09:39,400 --> 00:09:41,620
But muscle can't compete with machinery.

194
00:09:42,700 --> 00:09:46,200
While a farmer and his scythe can cut maybe two acres of wheat per day,

195
00:09:48,040 --> 00:09:52,360
today's modern combines can cut the same two acres in 12 minutes or less.

196
00:09:54,100 --> 00:09:59,640
And in just nine seconds, a combine harvests enough wheat to make 70 loaves of bread.

197
00:10:00,300 --> 00:10:03,540
And the world demands plenty of bread.

198
00:10:04,440 --> 00:10:08,280
Next to corn, wheat is the second most cultivated cereal grain in the world.

199
00:10:08,940 --> 00:10:13,180
Wheat is grown virtually everywhere, and in any given month of the year,

200
00:10:13,360 --> 00:10:15,220
wheat is being harvested somewhere in the world.

201
00:10:16,120 --> 00:10:20,040
More than 60 million acres of wheat are harvested each year in the United States.

202
00:10:21,640 --> 00:10:26,420
Out of the 42 wheat-growing states, Kansas typically ranks number one.

203
00:10:26,421 --> 00:10:30,020
In fact, nearly one-fifth of all US wheat is Kansas grown.

204
00:10:30,120 --> 00:10:33,360
North Dakota is a close second.

205
00:10:33,460 --> 00:10:36,560
Kansas grows about 10 million acres per year of wheat,

206
00:10:36,660 --> 00:10:37,860
which is a lot of wheat.

207
00:10:37,960 --> 00:10:41,260
But the world as a whole grows about 500 million acres

208
00:10:41,360 --> 00:10:43,000
per year of wheat.

209
00:10:43,100 --> 00:10:45,000
The number one producer in the world, surprisingly,

210
00:10:45,100 --> 00:10:47,580
is actually China.

211
00:10:47,680 --> 00:10:50,520
China produces more than 90 million tons of wheat

212
00:10:50,620 --> 00:10:54,180
each year, and India generates 70 million tons.

213
00:10:54,181 --> 00:10:58,180
The United States' 60 million tons ranks third, followed by

214
00:10:58,280 --> 00:11:02,120
Russia, France, and Canada.

215
00:11:02,220 --> 00:11:06,120
Even so, the United States is the largest wheat exporter.

216
00:11:06,220 --> 00:11:10,220
Nearly 50% of the crop is shipped overseas each year.

217
00:11:10,320 --> 00:11:12,740
Wheat provides approximately 20% of the food needs

218
00:11:12,840 --> 00:11:13,840
of the world.

219
00:11:13,940 --> 00:11:17,240
One acre will produce enough wheat to feed 9,000 people

220
00:11:17,340 --> 00:11:18,640
for one day.

221
00:11:18,740 --> 00:11:21,740
The Port of Portland in Oregon exports the largest

222
00:11:21,741 --> 00:11:24,840
largest amount of United States wheat.

223
00:11:24,940 --> 00:11:28,040
Four million tons pass through Columbia Grain's Terminal 5

224
00:11:28,140 --> 00:11:30,880
export elevator each year.

225
00:11:30,980 --> 00:11:33,980
The grain that we're going to get here is going to go all

226
00:11:34,080 --> 00:11:34,980
over the world.

227
00:11:35,080 --> 00:11:38,500
Our primary customers are the Far Eastern flour millers,

228
00:11:38,600 --> 00:11:42,100
Japan being a very big customer.

229
00:11:42,200 --> 00:11:45,600
The facility unloads about 75% of the wheat by rail car.

230
00:11:45,601 --> 00:11:50,600
Each one of these cars really takes only about five minutes

231
00:11:50,700 --> 00:11:52,540
to unload.

232
00:11:52,640 --> 00:11:56,840
The weed is going to fall into the rail receiving pit here,

233
00:11:56,940 --> 00:12:00,440
and then it's going to access some conveying systems

234
00:12:00,540 --> 00:12:03,520
to get to bucket elevator legs, where we'll take it

235
00:12:03,620 --> 00:12:07,020
to the top of the elevator and then distribute it for storage

236
00:12:07,120 --> 00:12:10,160
into one of the storage silos.

237
00:12:10,260 --> 00:12:13,360
With a storage capacity of 100,000 tons,

238
00:12:13,361 --> 00:12:14,360
tons.

239
00:12:14,460 --> 00:12:17,360
The inventory may be emptied and refilled up to five times

240
00:12:17,460 --> 00:12:18,160
each month.

241
00:12:21,060 --> 00:12:24,600
Two conveyor belts carry the grain at 900 tons per hour,

242
00:12:24,700 --> 00:12:28,900
from the silos out to the ship for transport.

243
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,420
We're loading hard red winter wheat, and this hatch,

244
00:12:31,520 --> 00:12:34,480
it's going to hold about 10,000 tons, which

245
00:12:34,580 --> 00:12:38,560
is equivalent to the size of one of those 110 car shuttle

246
00:12:38,660 --> 00:12:39,560
trains.

247
00:12:39,660 --> 00:12:42,560
We started loading this vessel about two in the morning

248
00:12:42,561 --> 00:12:48,460
today, and it'll take us a couple days to finish it.

249
00:12:48,560 --> 00:12:50,560
But this worldwide staple wasn't always

250
00:12:50,660 --> 00:12:54,300
so widely distributed.

251
00:12:54,400 --> 00:12:58,980
Wheat originated as a wild grass food more than 16,000 years ago

252
00:12:59,080 --> 00:13:02,880
in Southwest Asia in the area known as the Fertile Crescent,

253
00:13:02,980 --> 00:13:06,620
modern-day Syria and Iraq.

254
00:13:06,720 --> 00:13:10,120
Nomads gathered the so-called wild einkorn wheat,

255
00:13:10,121 --> 00:13:13,420
separated the kernels, and ate them raw.

256
00:13:13,520 --> 00:13:17,020
Around 7,000 BC, they discovered that planting the grain yielded

257
00:13:17,120 --> 00:13:19,860
yet more seeds.

258
00:13:19,960 --> 00:13:22,600
And it was these seeds of wheat that became the seeds

259
00:13:22,700 --> 00:13:24,000
of civilization.

260
00:13:24,100 --> 00:13:26,880
People no longer were forced to migrate around looking

261
00:13:26,980 --> 00:13:28,380
for wild stands of wheat.

262
00:13:28,480 --> 00:13:30,740
It really was the genesis of our civilization

263
00:13:30,840 --> 00:13:33,740
that people were able to become full-time farmers.

264
00:13:33,840 --> 00:13:37,180
By 5,000 BC, wheat farming had spread throughout Asia

265
00:13:37,280 --> 00:13:40,120
and into Europe and North Africa.

266
00:13:40,121 --> 00:13:43,960
Soon, farmers began to cook and grind the grains.

267
00:13:44,060 --> 00:13:48,260
When people first started grinding whole grains into

268
00:13:48,360 --> 00:13:52,760
flour, this was as advanced as it got.

269
00:13:52,860 --> 00:13:55,360
By adding water to the coarse ground flour,

270
00:13:55,460 --> 00:13:57,980
farmers could create porridge.

271
00:13:58,080 --> 00:14:00,080
And many attribute the origin of bread making

272
00:14:00,180 --> 00:14:02,880
to a serendipitous event when someone left their wheat porridge

273
00:14:02,980 --> 00:14:06,280
out in the hot sun.

274
00:14:06,281 --> 00:14:09,920
It's a primitive method that remains tried and true.

275
00:14:10,020 --> 00:14:12,620
So we put that flatbread in the oven about 20 minutes ago.

276
00:14:12,720 --> 00:14:13,820
Let's see what we have.

277
00:14:17,820 --> 00:14:21,520
Well, there it is.

278
00:14:21,620 --> 00:14:23,840
Nicely browned on the sides.

279
00:14:23,940 --> 00:14:24,640
Pretty light.

280
00:14:28,140 --> 00:14:30,640
Smells weedy.

281
00:14:30,740 --> 00:14:33,640
Let's see what happens when we break it open.

282
00:14:33,641 --> 00:14:37,340
Very dark, a little moist.

283
00:14:37,440 --> 00:14:39,640
Probably could have been thinner,

284
00:14:39,740 --> 00:14:41,880
but looks good enough to eat.

285
00:14:44,580 --> 00:14:46,380
A little crunchy.

286
00:14:46,480 --> 00:14:49,180
Tastes great.

287
00:14:49,280 --> 00:14:53,000
I can see why people did this for thousands of years.

288
00:14:53,100 --> 00:14:55,800
By chance, wild airborne yeast found its way

289
00:14:55,900 --> 00:15:00,740
into an ancient wheat mixture, creating leavened bread.

290
00:15:00,741 --> 00:15:04,380
And the rest is delicious history.

291
00:15:04,480 --> 00:15:06,840
Today, more than 70% of harvested wheat

292
00:15:06,940 --> 00:15:10,280
is used to make bread.

293
00:15:10,380 --> 00:15:14,380
Whether baking baguettes, ciabatta, or sourdough,

294
00:15:14,480 --> 00:15:18,700
the staff of life is fashioned from wheat.

295
00:15:18,800 --> 00:15:20,700
So is pasta.

296
00:15:20,800 --> 00:15:26,200
But if Marco Polo didn't bring it to Italy, who did?

297
00:15:26,300 --> 00:15:27,900
The world grows enough wheat each year

298
00:15:27,901 --> 00:15:30,900
to fill a line of trucks wrapped around the Earth

299
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,500
almost 2 and 1 1 times.

300
00:15:33,600 --> 00:15:35,600
Wheat will return on Modern Marvels.

301
00:15:40,140 --> 00:15:42,640
We now return to Wheat on Modern Marvels.

302
00:15:44,760 --> 00:15:47,260
Ooh, hot.

303
00:15:47,360 --> 00:15:49,660
Among the grains found in food products,

304
00:15:49,760 --> 00:15:52,660
wheat takes the cake and makes the cake.

305
00:15:52,760 --> 00:15:55,400
It's in more than 3,000 foods.

306
00:15:55,500 --> 00:15:57,900
But before these items make it to the supermarket shelf,

307
00:15:57,901 --> 00:16:02,900
wheat undergoes a high-tech journey from field to flower.

308
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:07,080
The Conagra Mills plant in Decatur, Alabama,

309
00:16:07,180 --> 00:16:11,580
grinds more than 1.6 million pounds of flower every day.

310
00:16:14,480 --> 00:16:17,960
About 50% of our wheat comes in via barge,

311
00:16:18,060 --> 00:16:21,120
and this particular barge has 50,000 bushels of grain on it,

312
00:16:21,220 --> 00:16:22,660
which to put in perspective would

313
00:16:22,760 --> 00:16:25,660
be roughly 3.6 million loaves of bread.

314
00:16:25,661 --> 00:16:26,660
bread.

315
00:16:26,760 --> 00:16:29,000
We have two power shovels that are pulling the wheat

316
00:16:29,100 --> 00:16:32,900
towards the center of the barge where we have a elevator leg

317
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,300
that pulls the wheat up to the conveyor that pulls it back

318
00:16:35,400 --> 00:16:37,500
to our flat storage.

319
00:16:37,600 --> 00:16:40,200
We can unload 6,200 bushels an hour

320
00:16:40,300 --> 00:16:42,220
using those shovels.

321
00:16:42,320 --> 00:16:46,820
Each bushel of wheat contains a million kernels.

322
00:16:46,920 --> 00:16:50,760
Each kernel is made up of three parts, the outer shell called

323
00:16:50,860 --> 00:16:55,660
bran, full of fiber, the embryo called germ, high in protein,

324
00:16:55,661 --> 00:16:59,360
protein, and the nutritive tissue called endosperm,

325
00:16:59,361 --> 00:17:01,060
high in starch.

326
00:17:01,061 --> 00:17:03,840
Whole grain flour is made from the entire kernel,

327
00:17:03,841 --> 00:17:07,340
while white flour is made from the endosperm only.

328
00:17:07,341 --> 00:17:09,680
Out of 100 pounds of wheat, we'll

329
00:17:09,681 --> 00:17:13,880
make about 75 pounds of refined white flour,

330
00:17:13,881 --> 00:17:18,340
about 23 pounds of milfied, which contains the bran,

331
00:17:18,341 --> 00:17:23,760
and about 2 pounds of wheat germ.

332
00:17:23,761 --> 00:17:26,360
The process begins with a rigorous cleaning routine

333
00:17:26,361 --> 00:17:29,700
to remove any debris from the grain.

334
00:17:29,701 --> 00:17:34,000
From there, the colonels enter one of 28 roller mills.

335
00:17:34,001 --> 00:17:36,700
Each roller mill has a slightly different purpose.

336
00:17:36,701 --> 00:17:39,900
The stock comes in the top, comes down through the machine,

337
00:17:39,901 --> 00:17:42,980
it's ground, and discharges the bottom.

338
00:17:42,981 --> 00:17:45,280
This is the first brake roller mill.

339
00:17:45,281 --> 00:17:47,480
Here, whole wheat comes into the machine,

340
00:17:47,481 --> 00:17:49,320
and it's ground for the first time.

341
00:17:49,321 --> 00:17:53,580
The rollers share the wheat, break it open,

342
00:17:53,581 --> 00:17:54,820
Prepare it for sifting.

343
00:18:00,120 --> 00:18:02,320
A complex array of pressurized tubes

344
00:18:02,321 --> 00:18:05,200
carries the grain from floor to floor.

345
00:18:05,201 --> 00:18:07,520
We're here on the fifth floor of the mill.

346
00:18:07,521 --> 00:18:10,600
The purpose of the sifter is to separate the ground mill

347
00:18:10,601 --> 00:18:13,140
stocks by particle size.

348
00:18:13,141 --> 00:18:16,640
This is done with a nest of sieves inside each sifter

349
00:18:16,641 --> 00:18:17,500
section.

350
00:18:17,501 --> 00:18:20,780
The sifter swings in about a 3 and 1 half inch circle

351
00:18:20,781 --> 00:18:23,580
at about 240 RPM.

352
00:18:23,581 --> 00:18:27,180
As the sifter rotates, the sieves separate the ground grain

353
00:18:27,280 --> 00:18:28,620
by size.

354
00:18:28,720 --> 00:18:30,780
The larger particles remain at the top,

355
00:18:30,880 --> 00:18:34,020
while the smaller ones fall to the bottom.

356
00:18:34,120 --> 00:18:36,920
The coarse brownie particles will go to brake rolls,

357
00:18:37,020 --> 00:18:41,240
will be ground with roller mills that have teeth,

358
00:18:41,340 --> 00:18:44,840
and the endosperm containing white, fine particles

359
00:18:44,940 --> 00:18:46,940
will go to reduction rolls, which are smooth,

360
00:18:47,040 --> 00:18:50,540
but will grind hard into flour.

361
00:18:50,640 --> 00:18:52,540
The kernels pass through these rollers

362
00:18:52,541 --> 00:18:55,840
and sifters up to 15 times in just 20 minutes

363
00:18:55,940 --> 00:19:00,320
before heading off to packaging as white flour.

364
00:19:00,420 --> 00:19:03,720
Though this mechanized process is hundreds of years old,

365
00:19:03,820 --> 00:19:06,560
ConAgra has managed to put a new spin on it.

366
00:19:06,660 --> 00:19:08,920
We came up with a special milling process, which

367
00:19:09,020 --> 00:19:12,200
reduces the bran and the germ particles

368
00:19:12,300 --> 00:19:15,000
to the same particle size as the endosperm.

369
00:19:15,100 --> 00:19:16,860
The so-called ultragrain flour

370
00:19:16,960 --> 00:19:19,860
maintains the nutritional value of whole wheat.

371
00:19:19,960 --> 00:19:21,900
This is a traditional whole wheat bread.

372
00:19:21,901 --> 00:19:25,500
You can see the very visible brand specs in that.

373
00:19:25,600 --> 00:19:28,600
When you look at this 100% ultra-grain product,

374
00:19:28,700 --> 00:19:31,740
you can see that it's very much like a white bread product.

375
00:19:31,840 --> 00:19:33,740
It has a very soft texture.

376
00:19:33,840 --> 00:19:38,140
And when you eat it, it has the very same mouthfeel

377
00:19:38,240 --> 00:19:40,220
as some of the white breads that you find

378
00:19:40,320 --> 00:19:42,660
in the marketplace today.

379
00:19:42,760 --> 00:19:45,660
Whether it's ultra-grain, whole wheat, or white,

380
00:19:45,760 --> 00:19:49,560
choosing the right flour for your baked goods is essential.

381
00:19:49,561 --> 00:19:52,960
That's because flour can be ground from two types of wheat,

382
00:19:53,060 --> 00:19:55,300
hard or soft.

383
00:19:55,400 --> 00:19:58,540
In fact, the secret to wheat's superior bread-making ability

384
00:19:58,640 --> 00:20:02,040
is gluten, a protein that is formed when water is added

385
00:20:02,140 --> 00:20:05,180
to wheat flour and mixed into a dough.

386
00:20:05,280 --> 00:20:09,740
The gluten gives bread its volume and structure.

387
00:20:09,840 --> 00:20:13,220
It's the key component in the grain that makes doughs stretchy

388
00:20:13,320 --> 00:20:16,360
and holds baked goods together.

389
00:20:16,361 --> 00:20:19,260
Hard wheat contains lots of gluten.

390
00:20:19,360 --> 00:20:22,100
These wheats are used to produce bread and rolls.

391
00:20:22,200 --> 00:20:24,300
Soft wheat contains little gluten,

392
00:20:24,400 --> 00:20:26,300
so the products don't stretch as much,

393
00:20:26,400 --> 00:20:29,600
becoming flaky and tender.

394
00:20:29,700 --> 00:20:32,600
These wheats are used in crackers, cakes, and pastries.

395
00:20:35,720 --> 00:20:37,620
But it takes a special kind of wheat

396
00:20:37,720 --> 00:20:40,620
to make the quintessential Italian dish.

397
00:20:40,720 --> 00:20:43,680
Durum is an especially hard wheat variety

398
00:20:43,681 --> 00:20:46,580
variety used to create pasta.

399
00:20:46,680 --> 00:20:49,880
Americans consume more than 6 billion pounds of pasta each

400
00:20:49,980 --> 00:20:52,680
year.

401
00:20:52,780 --> 00:20:55,620
New World Pasta is the leading dry pasta manufacturer

402
00:20:55,720 --> 00:20:57,920
in the United States.

403
00:20:58,020 --> 00:21:00,120
At their plant in Winchester, Virginia,

404
00:21:00,220 --> 00:21:05,040
they make oodles and oodles of noodles 24-7.

405
00:21:05,140 --> 00:21:08,180
We make over 600,000 pounds of product in a day,

406
00:21:08,280 --> 00:21:11,380
or up to 200 million pounds in a year.

407
00:21:11,381 --> 00:21:14,320
We make several different brands of pasta here,

408
00:21:14,420 --> 00:21:18,180
but our most popular are our Razzoni brands.

409
00:21:18,280 --> 00:21:21,220
Directly behind me is our lasagna production.

410
00:21:21,320 --> 00:21:24,720
We have seven production lines where we can make up

411
00:21:24,820 --> 00:21:28,060
to 52 different shapes.

412
00:21:28,160 --> 00:21:32,740
The process starts with taking semolina flour, adding water,

413
00:21:32,840 --> 00:21:33,840
mixing it.

414
00:21:33,940 --> 00:21:38,840
The mixing process will take anywhere from 15 to 20 minutes.

415
00:21:38,940 --> 00:21:41,380
Semolina is the coarsely ground endosperm

416
00:21:41,381 --> 00:21:42,920
of durum wheat.

417
00:21:43,020 --> 00:21:45,380
It has two key essential characteristics,

418
00:21:45,480 --> 00:21:47,220
which are color and tenacity.

419
00:21:47,320 --> 00:21:50,060
The color comes from the naturally occurring pigments

420
00:21:50,160 --> 00:21:53,060
in the wheat, and that translates into the golden color

421
00:21:53,160 --> 00:21:54,960
that we normally see in pasta.

422
00:21:55,060 --> 00:21:57,700
The tenacity comes from the wheat gluten, which

423
00:21:57,800 --> 00:22:01,760
is the protein in the wheat, and that provides the strength

424
00:22:01,860 --> 00:22:04,140
of the product.

425
00:22:04,240 --> 00:22:06,740
Under Italian law, pasta can only

426
00:22:06,840 --> 00:22:11,380
be made from 100% durum wheat semolina flour and water.

427
00:22:11,381 --> 00:22:13,420
Even though there's not a similar law in the United

428
00:22:13,520 --> 00:22:17,260
States, New World pasta still adheres to Italy's strict pasta

429
00:22:17,360 --> 00:22:19,360
principle.

430
00:22:19,460 --> 00:22:22,060
After mixing, we extrude the product.

431
00:22:22,160 --> 00:22:25,260
During the extrusion process, we are forcing that dough

432
00:22:25,360 --> 00:22:26,360
through a die.

433
00:22:26,460 --> 00:22:29,000
And that die gives the product a particular shape,

434
00:22:29,100 --> 00:22:33,440
similar to what we did as a kid using Play-Doh.

435
00:22:33,540 --> 00:22:35,440
But these industrial Play-Doh machines

436
00:22:35,540 --> 00:22:37,640
aren't kid-friendly.

437
00:22:37,740 --> 00:22:40,840
Razor-sharp blades rotating beneath the metal dies,

438
00:22:40,841 --> 00:22:42,540
slice and dice the pasta to size.

439
00:22:45,440 --> 00:22:48,540
Once cut, the pasta makes its way to massive dryers

440
00:22:48,640 --> 00:22:52,080
that circulate hot, moist air around up to 30,000 pounds

441
00:22:52,180 --> 00:22:54,880
of product at a time.

442
00:22:54,980 --> 00:22:57,960
Temperatures will reach up to 200 degrees Fahrenheit.

443
00:22:58,060 --> 00:23:01,220
Our short goods products take about three hours to dry,

444
00:23:01,320 --> 00:23:05,400
where long goods products take up to five hours to dry.

445
00:23:05,500 --> 00:23:08,140
Packaging is a modern labyrinth of conveyors,

446
00:23:08,240 --> 00:23:10,840
buckets, and chutes.

447
00:23:10,841 --> 00:23:16,140
overflowing with spaghetti, penne, and rotini.

448
00:23:16,240 --> 00:23:18,640
Wheat's long journey to the dinner table as pasta

449
00:23:18,740 --> 00:23:22,580
can be traced as far back as 2000 BC.

450
00:23:22,680 --> 00:23:26,680
But pasta's origins are shrouded in mystery and myth.

451
00:23:26,780 --> 00:23:29,520
Contrary to popular belief, Marco Polo

452
00:23:29,620 --> 00:23:32,160
did not bring pasta to Italy.

453
00:23:32,260 --> 00:23:34,560
Most historians believe it was introduced to Italy

454
00:23:34,660 --> 00:23:39,660
during the Arab conquests of Sicily in the 8th century.

455
00:23:39,661 --> 00:23:43,860
Today, there are more than 600 pasta shapes worldwide.

456
00:23:43,960 --> 00:23:47,800
While Americans eat an average of 20 pounds of pasta annually,

457
00:23:47,900 --> 00:23:50,400
that's only a third of what their Italian counterparts

458
00:23:50,500 --> 00:23:52,400
consume.

459
00:23:52,500 --> 00:23:55,300
Cooking pasta is as simple as boiling water,

460
00:23:55,400 --> 00:23:58,120
but it takes a keen eye to get it just right.

461
00:23:58,220 --> 00:24:00,020
I like it a little al dente.

462
00:24:00,120 --> 00:24:02,020
That means to the tooth.

463
00:24:02,120 --> 00:24:06,720
You can usually tell when it's al dente by looking at the ends

464
00:24:06,721 --> 00:24:10,420
ends of the pasta, and you'll see a tiny little white core

465
00:24:10,520 --> 00:24:13,320
that's not cooked as well as the surrounding area

466
00:24:13,420 --> 00:24:14,420
of the pasta.

467
00:24:14,520 --> 00:24:17,960
And it gives it that unique little bite and cleanliness

468
00:24:18,060 --> 00:24:20,960
when you're eating it.

469
00:24:21,060 --> 00:24:22,560
Perfecto.

470
00:24:22,660 --> 00:24:24,480
Molto bene, as they would say in Italy.

471
00:24:24,580 --> 00:24:25,880
It's perfect.

472
00:24:25,980 --> 00:24:30,080
While wheat lovers worldwide are piling on the pasta,

473
00:24:30,180 --> 00:24:35,180
others are turning to wheat beer to wash it down.

474
00:24:35,181 --> 00:24:38,180
But why is it hazy instead of clear like other beers?

475
00:24:40,580 --> 00:24:43,180
More than 2 thirds of the durum wheat grown in the United

476
00:24:43,280 --> 00:24:45,420
States comes from North Dakota.

477
00:24:45,520 --> 00:24:48,020
The state produces enough durum wheat annually

478
00:24:48,120 --> 00:24:51,940
to make 13.7 billion servings of spaghetti.

479
00:24:52,040 --> 00:24:54,540
Wheat will return on Modern Marvels.

480
00:24:59,440 --> 00:25:02,040
We now return to Wheat on Modern Marvels.

481
00:25:05,180 --> 00:25:07,180
Beer.

482
00:25:07,280 --> 00:25:10,880
35 billion gallons of the frothy brew are sold worldwide each

483
00:25:10,980 --> 00:25:12,180
year.

484
00:25:12,280 --> 00:25:15,180
Americans chug down more than 15 million gallons of the stuff

485
00:25:15,280 --> 00:25:17,180
each day.

486
00:25:17,280 --> 00:25:20,180
Barley is the grain used to make most beers.

487
00:25:20,280 --> 00:25:23,180
But at the Widmer Brothers Brewery in Portland, Oregon,

488
00:25:23,280 --> 00:25:25,180
wheat has taken over.

489
00:25:25,280 --> 00:25:29,180
Since 1986, the brewery has been home to America's original

490
00:25:29,280 --> 00:25:32,180
Hefeweizen, or Unleashed Brewery.

491
00:25:32,181 --> 00:25:35,620
to America's original Hefeweizen, or unfiltered wheat

492
00:25:35,720 --> 00:25:36,420
beer.

493
00:25:38,920 --> 00:25:43,120
It's a brew that first became popular in 15th century Bavaria

494
00:25:43,220 --> 00:25:45,820
and currently makes up almost a quarter of beer sales

495
00:25:45,920 --> 00:25:46,720
in Germany.

496
00:25:48,940 --> 00:25:51,640
Wheat beer's unique style is also catching on in America.

497
00:25:54,240 --> 00:25:54,940
Yum.

498
00:25:54,941 --> 00:25:56,140
That's pretty good.

499
00:25:56,240 --> 00:25:59,500
Wheat contributes sort of a nice aromatic fresh bread

500
00:25:59,600 --> 00:26:01,780
profile, which we find very appealing.

501
00:26:01,781 --> 00:26:03,280
It also contributes mouthfeel.

502
00:26:03,380 --> 00:26:05,880
It gives the beer more substance on your tongue

503
00:26:05,980 --> 00:26:08,580
and on your palate.

504
00:26:08,680 --> 00:26:11,320
Each week, brewing brothers Kurt and Rob Widmer

505
00:26:11,420 --> 00:26:16,820
turn 75,000 pounds of wheat into more than 125,000 gallons

506
00:26:16,920 --> 00:26:19,340
of beer.

507
00:26:19,440 --> 00:26:22,800
Last year, we brewed about 350,000 barrels

508
00:26:22,900 --> 00:26:24,000
out of this facility.

509
00:26:24,100 --> 00:26:26,440
This is kind of the hot end of our brew house.

510
00:26:26,540 --> 00:26:31,780
And we can brew six or seven times a day in a 24-hour period.

511
00:26:31,781 --> 00:26:34,780
The bottom line of brewing beer is to convert the grain sugars

512
00:26:34,880 --> 00:26:37,920
into alcohol and carbon dioxide.

513
00:26:38,020 --> 00:26:41,020
The process starts with this tank called a mash mixer.

514
00:26:41,120 --> 00:26:42,520
That's where you take malted wheat

515
00:26:42,620 --> 00:26:45,220
and soak it in warm water.

516
00:26:45,320 --> 00:26:47,520
Malted wheat is grain that has been soaked in water

517
00:26:47,620 --> 00:26:50,340
to the point of germination.

518
00:26:50,440 --> 00:26:53,840
This causes the grain to produce enzymes that convert its starch

519
00:26:53,940 --> 00:26:56,040
into sugar.

520
00:26:56,140 --> 00:26:58,340
Even though this brew is Hefeweizen,

521
00:26:58,440 --> 00:27:01,780
Widmer uses malted barley for half the mixture.

522
00:27:01,781 --> 00:27:06,080
The enzymatic power of the grain to convert starch to sugar

523
00:27:06,180 --> 00:27:09,580
is less than wheat than barley, so you really do need the barley

524
00:27:09,680 --> 00:27:10,960
there.

525
00:27:11,060 --> 00:27:13,260
The mash mixture spins for an hour

526
00:27:13,360 --> 00:27:16,100
at 126 degrees Fahrenheit.

527
00:27:16,200 --> 00:27:20,000
And then we ramp that up, ending at about 160 degrees,

528
00:27:20,100 --> 00:27:23,300
and then we call mash off, which is at 170,

529
00:27:23,400 --> 00:27:28,140
and that stops the enzymatic conversion of starch to sugar.

530
00:27:28,240 --> 00:27:29,700
The mash is then pumped

531
00:27:29,701 --> 00:27:33,240
pumped into a so-called louder ton, in which the solution of

532
00:27:33,340 --> 00:27:36,340
water and sugars, called wort, is drained from the mash

533
00:27:36,440 --> 00:27:39,140
through a sieve-like bottom.

534
00:27:39,240 --> 00:27:42,240
A mechanical rotating rake ensures the mash remains

535
00:27:42,340 --> 00:27:44,740
fluid throughout the process.

536
00:27:44,840 --> 00:27:47,960
That's about 170 degrees, and it takes about two hours

537
00:27:48,060 --> 00:27:49,860
to complete the loudering.

538
00:27:49,960 --> 00:27:51,860
This is a tricky part of the process when you're brewing

539
00:27:51,960 --> 00:27:55,260
wheat beer, because wheat has a higher protein content,

540
00:27:55,360 --> 00:27:58,200
and it tends to, to use a technical term,

541
00:27:58,201 --> 00:27:59,800
gum up the works.

542
00:27:59,900 --> 00:28:02,400
And if you're not kind of on top of it all the time,

543
00:28:02,500 --> 00:28:05,900
you can have a stuck mash where the liquid won't flow through.

544
00:28:06,000 --> 00:28:08,740
This tank does a great job of making sure that we're able to

545
00:28:08,840 --> 00:28:12,940
rinse all the flavor and color and sugar out of the grain.

546
00:28:13,040 --> 00:28:15,460
The remaining wheat and barley head to a local farm

547
00:28:15,560 --> 00:28:18,660
to become cattle feed, while the liquid continues onto the

548
00:28:18,760 --> 00:28:20,760
kettle for boiling.

549
00:28:20,860 --> 00:28:22,260
So we're done with the grain.

550
00:28:22,360 --> 00:28:24,700
We've just got the sweet grain tea called wort.

551
00:28:24,800 --> 00:28:26,960
And it's here that hops are introduced.

552
00:28:26,961 --> 00:28:31,200
These are baled hops, and what we do is get them pulverized

553
00:28:31,300 --> 00:28:33,700
and pelletized.

554
00:28:33,800 --> 00:28:36,600
Hops are flowering plants that give beer its bitterness,

555
00:28:36,700 --> 00:28:39,400
aroma, and flavor.

556
00:28:39,500 --> 00:28:41,800
Wheat beers tend to have a low amount of hops

557
00:28:41,900 --> 00:28:45,220
and a high concentration of carbon dioxide.

558
00:28:45,320 --> 00:28:48,020
That means a light flavor with lots of bubbles,

559
00:28:48,120 --> 00:28:51,120
making it a popular summertime brew.

560
00:28:51,220 --> 00:28:53,760
But where does that golden haze come from?

561
00:28:53,860 --> 00:28:55,720
There are proteins that we extract

562
00:28:55,721 --> 00:28:59,620
from the wheat malt that go all the way through to the glass

563
00:28:59,720 --> 00:29:01,020
that we think is an essential part

564
00:29:01,120 --> 00:29:03,320
of how the beer should look.

565
00:29:03,420 --> 00:29:05,560
Since Hefeweizen is unfiltered,

566
00:29:05,660 --> 00:29:07,760
the proteins from the wheat remain suspended

567
00:29:07,860 --> 00:29:11,260
in the end product. These long molecular chains

568
00:29:11,360 --> 00:29:14,580
are difficult to break apart, creating both a cloudy

569
00:29:14,680 --> 00:29:18,080
appearance and the distinctive thick, foamy head of the beer.

570
00:29:21,220 --> 00:29:22,980
As a result, a bottle of Hefeweizen

571
00:29:22,981 --> 00:29:26,120
and offers the same amount of protein and carbohydrates

572
00:29:26,220 --> 00:29:29,320
as a slice of bread.

573
00:29:29,420 --> 00:29:33,420
Back in the brew house, the wort boils for 75 minutes.

574
00:29:33,520 --> 00:29:34,520
But it's still not beer yet.

575
00:29:34,620 --> 00:29:36,020
There's no alcohol present.

576
00:29:36,120 --> 00:29:38,520
But we've set up the absolute perfect conditions

577
00:29:38,620 --> 00:29:43,440
for fermentation, and that's started by adding our yeast.

578
00:29:43,540 --> 00:29:44,940
Yeast is the magical ingredient

579
00:29:45,040 --> 00:29:50,640
that converts the sugars into alcohol and carbon dioxide.

580
00:29:50,641 --> 00:29:52,980
Kurt was introduced to the yeast that we use

581
00:29:52,981 --> 00:29:56,540
at a small brew pub in Dusseldorf, Germany.

582
00:29:56,541 --> 00:30:00,280
But in 16th century Germany, wheat's esteemed status

583
00:30:00,281 --> 00:30:03,680
was grounds for the grain to be banned from beer.

584
00:30:03,681 --> 00:30:06,380
The German purity law, the Reinheitsgebot,

585
00:30:06,381 --> 00:30:11,500
dictated that beer could only contain malt, hops, water.

586
00:30:11,501 --> 00:30:13,200
And they didn't know about yeast at that time,

587
00:30:13,201 --> 00:30:14,640
but it had to be those ingredients.

588
00:30:14,641 --> 00:30:17,600
And that was specifically malted barley at that point.

589
00:30:17,601 --> 00:30:21,040
And they prescribed that law partially to restrict people

590
00:30:21,140 --> 00:30:23,940
from using wheat in beer so that there was more wheat

591
00:30:24,040 --> 00:30:25,840
available for bread.

592
00:30:25,940 --> 00:30:27,840
It wasn't until the 20th century

593
00:30:27,940 --> 00:30:32,440
that brewing wheat beer was once again permitted in Germany.

594
00:30:32,540 --> 00:30:34,220
The Widmer Brothers Brewery houses

595
00:30:34,320 --> 00:30:37,180
six 50-foot tall fermentation tanks,

596
00:30:37,280 --> 00:30:41,120
each with a 1,500-barrel capacity.

597
00:30:41,220 --> 00:30:43,420
So from here, the beer ferments,

598
00:30:43,520 --> 00:30:44,920
and it can be as long as a month,

599
00:30:45,020 --> 00:30:47,600
depending on the beer type that we're making.

600
00:30:47,601 --> 00:30:49,600
The fermentation is a fairly rapid style,

601
00:30:49,601 --> 00:30:51,600
and it's ready in about a week.

602
00:30:51,601 --> 00:30:55,600
Once it's finished, it's ready for either packaging into bottles

603
00:30:55,601 --> 00:30:58,600
or racking, which is beer in the kegging room.

604
00:30:58,601 --> 00:31:01,600
Here's where tradition meets technology.

605
00:31:01,601 --> 00:31:05,600
Two robots do all the heavy lifting that guys used to have to do.

606
00:31:05,601 --> 00:31:08,600
But one of the things that the robots do with our beer that's unique,

607
00:31:08,601 --> 00:31:11,600
because it's this unfiltered wheat beer,

608
00:31:11,601 --> 00:31:15,600
is that we flip our kegs because we found that when the bar owner clips them

609
00:31:15,601 --> 00:31:17,200
the bar owner clips them right side up again,

610
00:31:17,300 --> 00:31:18,880
that helps mix things up a little bit,

611
00:31:18,980 --> 00:31:20,500
and keeps the beer nice and turbid.

612
00:31:21,600 --> 00:31:23,640
Another way to bring out wheat beer's turbidity

613
00:31:23,740 --> 00:31:25,840
is with an authentic German pour.

614
00:31:27,140 --> 00:31:30,240
It's very important when a beer is poured

615
00:31:30,340 --> 00:31:36,220
that there be a nice creamy head for the duration of the beer

616
00:31:36,320 --> 00:31:37,680
as a person is drinking it.

617
00:31:37,780 --> 00:31:39,520
And that takes time.

618
00:31:39,620 --> 00:31:41,420
And so what I'm going to show you

619
00:31:41,520 --> 00:31:43,200
is how you would typically get it

620
00:31:43,201 --> 00:31:46,800
if you were in a small pub in Germany.

621
00:31:47,700 --> 00:31:50,400
Bartender would pour this, walk away,

622
00:31:50,401 --> 00:31:52,500
do whatever he has to do.

623
00:31:52,501 --> 00:31:55,240
Head dies down a little bit, they come back.

624
00:31:55,241 --> 00:31:57,320
And again, I'm pouring right down the center

625
00:31:57,321 --> 00:32:00,260
and I'm not making any effort to minimize the foam up.

626
00:32:00,261 --> 00:32:03,560
I want it to actually rise up and when I'm done,

627
00:32:03,561 --> 00:32:06,820
it should be a very thick head

628
00:32:06,821 --> 00:32:08,520
that you could balance a quarter on

629
00:32:08,521 --> 00:32:10,360
that stays there for the whole duration

630
00:32:10,361 --> 00:32:12,160
of the time that you're drinking that beer.

631
00:32:12,161 --> 00:32:14,160
God, that looks great, huh?

632
00:32:16,600 --> 00:32:17,600
Who could resist that?

633
00:32:20,860 --> 00:32:24,000
Whether guzzling or gobbling the grain,

634
00:32:24,100 --> 00:32:27,600
Americans consume an average of 134 pounds of wheat

635
00:32:27,700 --> 00:32:29,720
each year.

636
00:32:29,820 --> 00:32:32,320
As the worldwide population grows,

637
00:32:32,420 --> 00:32:36,120
our reliance on wheat only increases.

638
00:32:36,220 --> 00:32:38,920
But some experts fear the fragile crop could vanish

639
00:32:39,020 --> 00:32:41,920
in a matter of days.

640
00:32:41,921 --> 00:32:48,420
These scientists have turned to corn to prevent that from happening.

641
00:32:48,520 --> 00:32:50,320
According to the Beer Institute,

642
00:32:50,420 --> 00:32:53,560
brewers used more than 7.5 million pounds of wheat

643
00:32:53,660 --> 00:32:55,560
to make beer in 2000.

644
00:32:55,660 --> 00:32:58,940
By 2006, that number had nearly doubled

645
00:32:59,040 --> 00:33:02,480
to more than 14.5 million pounds of wheat.

646
00:33:02,580 --> 00:33:04,380
Wheat will return on Modern Marvels.

647
00:33:09,020 --> 00:33:11,580
We now return to Wheat on Modern Marvels.

648
00:33:11,581 --> 00:33:16,580
In the last 100 years, the Earth's population

649
00:33:16,680 --> 00:33:20,820
has quadrupled to more than 6.5 billion people.

650
00:33:20,920 --> 00:33:24,100
Billions are hungry every day, and hundreds of millions

651
00:33:24,200 --> 00:33:27,120
are in danger of starvation.

652
00:33:27,220 --> 00:33:30,340
Wheat has provided help in times of famine, epidemic

653
00:33:30,440 --> 00:33:32,700
disease, and war.

654
00:33:32,800 --> 00:33:36,400
But the future of this saving grain is at stake.

655
00:33:36,500 --> 00:33:39,400
Extreme weather, insects, and fungus

656
00:33:39,401 --> 00:33:43,700
and fungus threaten the survival of wheat fields worldwide.

657
00:33:43,800 --> 00:33:46,200
And a substantial loss of the wheat crop

658
00:33:46,300 --> 00:33:49,000
would be devastating to the global food supply.

659
00:33:49,100 --> 00:33:52,640
Historically, the world has operated usually

660
00:33:52,740 --> 00:33:55,340
in excess of 100 days of stored wheat.

661
00:33:55,440 --> 00:33:58,860
So if we just stop having wheat, we'd have at least 100 days

662
00:33:58,960 --> 00:34:00,460
to feed the world.

663
00:34:00,560 --> 00:34:03,160
And over the last, oh, say 10 years,

664
00:34:03,260 --> 00:34:06,500
that capacity has been diminishing down to something

665
00:34:06,600 --> 00:34:09,000
like 36 or 37 days.

666
00:34:09,001 --> 00:34:11,840
It is possible that we could have a disease that could

667
00:34:11,940 --> 00:34:16,440
devastate a crop in a matter of 10 or 15 days.

668
00:34:16,540 --> 00:34:20,980
In fact, the staff of life may be in danger.

669
00:34:21,080 --> 00:34:24,980
In March of 2008, a virulent strain of wheat stem rust

670
00:34:25,080 --> 00:34:30,660
called UG-99 that originated in Africa in 1999 spread into Iran,

671
00:34:30,760 --> 00:34:34,360
destroying vital wheat fields.

672
00:34:34,361 --> 00:34:40,000
And it's moved through Iran, and it's now threatening parts

673
00:34:40,100 --> 00:34:42,600
of the wheat crop in Pakistan, and eventually

674
00:34:42,700 --> 00:34:47,100
is going to threaten the wheat crops in India and even in China.

675
00:34:47,200 --> 00:34:50,600
And so it's really critical that breeders

676
00:34:50,700 --> 00:34:54,620
find new sources of resistance.

677
00:34:54,720 --> 00:34:57,120
At the Wheat Genetic and Genomic Resource Center

678
00:34:57,220 --> 00:34:59,880
at Kansas State University, scientists

679
00:34:59,980 --> 00:35:02,420
hope the key to resistance lies in one of wheat's

680
00:35:02,421 --> 00:35:04,820
ancient ancestors.

681
00:35:04,920 --> 00:35:07,260
In their gene bank devoted solely to wheat,

682
00:35:07,360 --> 00:35:09,660
more than 2,500 lines of the plant

683
00:35:09,760 --> 00:35:13,160
are maintained for such research purposes.

684
00:35:13,260 --> 00:35:16,160
We do a lot of screening for leaf rust resistance,

685
00:35:16,260 --> 00:35:18,160
and we've gone through 500 lines,

686
00:35:18,260 --> 00:35:21,080
and some are very resistant to the disease,

687
00:35:21,180 --> 00:35:24,080
and others are very susceptible to the disease.

688
00:35:24,180 --> 00:35:27,220
So unless you have a lot of lines to screen,

689
00:35:27,320 --> 00:35:30,780
you might not have the resistance that you need

690
00:35:30,781 --> 00:35:35,520
you need to transfer to wheat.

691
00:35:35,620 --> 00:35:38,180
And through the modern science of plant breeding,

692
00:35:38,280 --> 00:35:41,820
experts hope wheat will weather the storm.

693
00:35:41,920 --> 00:35:44,220
AgriPro has the largest wheat breeding program

694
00:35:44,320 --> 00:35:47,860
in North America. At their facility in Junction City,

695
00:35:47,960 --> 00:35:52,960
Kansas, it all starts with making crosses in the greenhouse.

696
00:35:53,060 --> 00:35:55,280
Wheat is a self-pollinating plant.

697
00:35:55,380 --> 00:35:59,580
Within each wheat floret are an embryo sac, a stigma,

698
00:35:59,581 --> 00:36:03,120
and anthers that contain pollen.

699
00:36:03,220 --> 00:36:06,280
When the plurith matures, the anthers open and release

700
00:36:06,380 --> 00:36:07,820
the pollen.

701
00:36:07,920 --> 00:36:11,060
It sticks to the stigma and germinates down into the embryo

702
00:36:11,160 --> 00:36:13,820
sac to pollinate the plant.

703
00:36:13,920 --> 00:36:18,300
The fertilized embryo ultimately becomes a new wheat kernel.

704
00:36:18,400 --> 00:36:20,960
So what I have to do here to make across the wheat

705
00:36:21,060 --> 00:36:25,640
is to take the anthers out so I just have the embryo left.

706
00:36:25,740 --> 00:36:29,440
After meticulously removing each minuscule anther,

707
00:36:29,441 --> 00:36:32,240
Researchers can then cross-breed the plant with other weeds that

708
00:36:32,340 --> 00:36:35,980
have superior qualities, such as disease resistance,

709
00:36:36,080 --> 00:36:39,280
high yields, and shorter growing seasons.

710
00:36:39,380 --> 00:36:43,680
Our goal is to produce around 1,000 to 1,500 crosses a year.

711
00:36:43,780 --> 00:36:46,520
Out of those 1,000 to 1,500, if we can have one to two

712
00:36:46,620 --> 00:36:49,560
varieties that make it to market that will be

713
00:36:49,660 --> 00:36:52,960
functionable to the farmer, we are very happy.

714
00:36:53,060 --> 00:36:57,960
And that journey to market takes 10 to 12 years of testing.

715
00:36:57,961 --> 00:37:01,400
Each year, AgriPro technicians plant the experimental varieties

716
00:37:01,500 --> 00:37:05,000
in one of their 5 feet by 10 feet test plots.

717
00:37:05,100 --> 00:37:07,840
At harvest, only the best are kept to be replanted

718
00:37:07,940 --> 00:37:10,100
the following season.

719
00:37:10,200 --> 00:37:13,940
We have close to 700 different plots of wheat.

720
00:37:14,040 --> 00:37:18,440
We will come into these plots, and we will select 100 to 150

721
00:37:18,540 --> 00:37:21,620
heads for good disease resistance and plant type.

722
00:37:21,720 --> 00:37:23,960
The purpose is to develop a variety

723
00:37:24,060 --> 00:37:27,960
that has a really good purity.

724
00:37:27,961 --> 00:37:32,100
Today, AgriPro offers 40 wheat varieties for farmers.

725
00:37:32,200 --> 00:37:36,600
Although each cost a whopping $1.2 million to develop,

726
00:37:36,700 --> 00:37:39,780
AgriPro sells about 9,000,000 50-pound bags of wheat

727
00:37:39,880 --> 00:37:43,940
in the United States and Canada each year.

728
00:37:44,040 --> 00:37:47,020
But perhaps the most notable achievements in wheat genetics

729
00:37:47,120 --> 00:37:50,780
dates back to the 1960s, when American plant breeder

730
00:37:50,880 --> 00:37:54,180
Norman Borlaug developed and introduced new wheat varieties

731
00:37:54,280 --> 00:37:57,960
to Mexico, India, and Pakistan.

732
00:37:57,961 --> 00:38:01,800
The short, high-yield wheat saved millions from starvation.

733
00:38:01,900 --> 00:38:04,300
It was so successful that it came to be called

734
00:38:04,400 --> 00:38:06,800
the Green Revolution.

735
00:38:06,900 --> 00:38:10,200
Now, once again, wheat is in for a big change

736
00:38:10,300 --> 00:38:14,200
with the help of an unlikely source, corn.

737
00:38:14,300 --> 00:38:17,140
AgriPro calls it speed breeding because the process

738
00:38:17,240 --> 00:38:20,180
takes half as long as traditional methods.

739
00:38:20,280 --> 00:38:22,220
Basically, we're taking a wheat plant

740
00:38:22,320 --> 00:38:24,320
and crossing it with a corn plant.

741
00:38:24,420 --> 00:38:27,260
And when we do that, you get a viable embryo.

742
00:38:27,261 --> 00:38:31,740
that developing embryo recognizes the corn chromosomes

743
00:38:31,840 --> 00:38:33,140
as being different.

744
00:38:33,240 --> 00:38:35,740
And so the cells of a wheat plant

745
00:38:35,840 --> 00:38:40,300
have a number of enzymes that are there to attack

746
00:38:40,400 --> 00:38:42,580
and destroy the corn chromosomes.

747
00:38:42,680 --> 00:38:47,080
And so what you end up with is a haploid wheat seedling.

748
00:38:47,180 --> 00:38:49,220
Haploid means the wheat embryo only

749
00:38:49,320 --> 00:38:51,820
has one set of chromosomes.

750
00:38:51,920 --> 00:38:55,120
So technicians double them through a chemical reaction,

751
00:38:55,121 --> 00:38:57,760
creating a pure wheat embryo.

752
00:38:57,860 --> 00:39:00,360
If they were left unattended, they

753
00:39:00,460 --> 00:39:02,620
would normally abort and die.

754
00:39:02,720 --> 00:39:08,940
So we have to go in and very carefully tease out that embryo

755
00:39:09,040 --> 00:39:11,000
and put it on an artificial media.

756
00:39:11,100 --> 00:39:13,740
That has all of the organics.

757
00:39:13,840 --> 00:39:18,380
It allows that young embryo to grow into a plant that

758
00:39:18,480 --> 00:39:20,440
produces seeds.

759
00:39:20,540 --> 00:39:22,240
Every kernel of the resulting plant

760
00:39:22,340 --> 00:39:24,120
will be genetically identical.

761
00:39:24,121 --> 00:39:25,920
With this and other methods, scientists

762
00:39:25,921 --> 00:39:28,820
are betting on a bright future for this vital crop.

763
00:39:28,821 --> 00:39:31,320
Breeders are going to be able to find wheat that produces

764
00:39:31,321 --> 00:39:34,360
much, much higher yields, that is basically impervious

765
00:39:34,361 --> 00:39:38,360
to all types of environmental or biological assaults.

766
00:39:38,361 --> 00:39:41,360
While many researchers strive for super wheat,

767
00:39:41,361 --> 00:39:44,080
one company hopes the golden grain could be the answer

768
00:39:44,081 --> 00:39:45,880
to saving millions of trees.

769
00:39:49,820 --> 00:39:52,520
Since 2005, an international organization

770
00:39:52,521 --> 00:39:55,060
has been working to sequence the wheat genome.

771
00:39:55,160 --> 00:39:57,960
With 16 billion base pairs of DNA,

772
00:39:58,060 --> 00:40:00,060
the wheat genome is five times larger

773
00:40:00,160 --> 00:40:01,860
than the human genome.

774
00:40:01,960 --> 00:40:04,260
Wheat will return on Modern Marvels.

775
00:40:09,280 --> 00:40:11,880
We now return to Wheat on Modern Marvels.

776
00:40:11,881 --> 00:40:12,780
Marvels.

777
00:40:21,920 --> 00:40:23,820
Can wheat rock?

778
00:40:23,920 --> 00:40:26,720
A company in Grand Island, Nebraska, thinks so.

779
00:40:26,820 --> 00:40:29,200
We saw a great opportunity to reach out to the world

780
00:40:29,300 --> 00:40:32,900
through music, teaching the world about biodegradables.

781
00:40:33,000 --> 00:40:34,660
We can actually make multiple products,

782
00:40:34,760 --> 00:40:36,400
including drumsticks and guitar picks,

783
00:40:36,500 --> 00:40:40,200
from different materials, such as wheat.

784
00:40:40,201 --> 00:40:44,300
Wheatware makes products from the entire wheat plant.

785
00:40:44,400 --> 00:40:46,740
Manufacturing starts with bioresin,

786
00:40:46,840 --> 00:40:49,780
a plastic-like material derived from the plant's sugars

787
00:40:49,880 --> 00:40:51,680
that can be molded into a myriad of shapes.

788
00:40:54,420 --> 00:40:56,380
But the Wheatware company is so secretive

789
00:40:56,480 --> 00:40:59,560
about its proprietary equipment that they covered most of it

790
00:40:59,660 --> 00:41:03,660
with blue tarps before the Modern Marbles team arrived.

791
00:41:03,760 --> 00:41:08,900
This technology is distinct from plastic injection molding.

792
00:41:08,901 --> 00:41:11,640
Plastic injection molding allows for high temperatures

793
00:41:11,740 --> 00:41:15,740
to melt the plastic pellets into a liquid,

794
00:41:15,840 --> 00:41:19,240
thereby pressurizing into a mold, whereas wheat

795
00:41:19,340 --> 00:41:21,540
is more like baking bread.

796
00:41:21,640 --> 00:41:24,140
In baking bread, if you have too high a heat,

797
00:41:24,240 --> 00:41:25,720
what you'll do is you'll burn it, which

798
00:41:25,820 --> 00:41:27,660
causes it to solidify.

799
00:41:27,760 --> 00:41:31,660
This technology is very specific.

800
00:41:31,760 --> 00:41:33,460
And wheatware keeps a very close guard

801
00:41:33,560 --> 00:41:34,360
on those specifics.

802
00:41:34,361 --> 00:41:38,360
Beyond their musical instruments,

803
00:41:38,460 --> 00:41:43,100
Wheatware offers golf products, eating utensils, hair combs,

804
00:41:43,200 --> 00:41:47,000
clothes hangers, and chopsticks, to name a few.

805
00:41:47,100 --> 00:41:49,000
We got involved with chopsticks when

806
00:41:49,100 --> 00:41:52,840
we heard the statistic that 25 million trees are clear cut

807
00:41:52,940 --> 00:41:56,520
each year to produce wood-disposable chopsticks.

808
00:41:56,620 --> 00:42:00,720
In Japan, they use 65 million chopsticks a day.

809
00:42:00,721 --> 00:42:04,560
So we realized that wheatware is heat resistant, strong,

810
00:42:04,660 --> 00:42:06,260
durable, and rigid.

811
00:42:06,360 --> 00:42:10,000
The quality is necessary for a chopstick replacement.

812
00:42:10,100 --> 00:42:12,740
We got busy.

813
00:42:12,840 --> 00:42:14,400
While similar products can be made

814
00:42:14,500 --> 00:42:18,480
from other crops like corn, wheat provides more durability

815
00:42:18,580 --> 00:42:19,600
and heat resistance.

816
00:42:23,100 --> 00:42:27,740
And this renewable resource will decompose in less than 90 days.

817
00:42:27,741 --> 00:42:32,040
That means golf tees can be left on the course as fertilizer.

818
00:42:32,140 --> 00:42:35,040
If golfing is not your thing, check out the world's first

819
00:42:35,140 --> 00:42:38,780
so-called eco-yo-yo made of wheat.

820
00:42:38,880 --> 00:42:42,180
Wheatware may have their wheat on a string,

821
00:42:42,280 --> 00:42:46,400
but it turns out there are plenty of other uses for the crop.

822
00:42:46,500 --> 00:42:50,000
The inedible parts of the plant, the wheat chap and straw,

823
00:42:50,100 --> 00:42:54,600
make up about half of the yield of the wheat crop.

824
00:42:54,601 --> 00:42:59,200
Many farmers use it as animal feed and bedding.

825
00:42:59,300 --> 00:43:02,400
It can even be transformed into cat litter, soap,

826
00:43:02,500 --> 00:43:05,440
and pillow stuffing.

827
00:43:05,540 --> 00:43:07,940
But one of the oldest uses for wheat straw

828
00:43:08,040 --> 00:43:10,140
is adobe construction.

829
00:43:10,240 --> 00:43:12,960
In fact, the first wheat farmers of the Fertile Crescent

830
00:43:13,060 --> 00:43:15,660
also turned to the plant to help build their cities.

831
00:43:17,860 --> 00:43:20,660
Bricks made from mud, clay, water, and straw

832
00:43:20,760 --> 00:43:23,200
are extremely durable.

833
00:43:23,201 --> 00:43:26,340
They can even be found in structures built more than 2,000

834
00:43:26,440 --> 00:43:28,340
years ago.

835
00:43:28,440 --> 00:43:32,840
This is a very archaic method of making a brick.

836
00:43:32,940 --> 00:43:36,140
Right now, we have sand and clay, and we're going to add a

837
00:43:36,240 --> 00:43:37,640
little straw.

838
00:43:37,740 --> 00:43:41,060
And as times progressed, people would put the straw on a block

839
00:43:41,160 --> 00:43:44,860
and chop it and make small fibers.

840
00:43:44,960 --> 00:43:48,860
The smaller the fibers, the stronger the brick.

841
00:43:48,960 --> 00:43:51,360
Though the straw makes up only about 1 sixth

842
00:43:51,361 --> 00:43:52,600
of each brick.

843
00:43:52,700 --> 00:43:54,200
It's the key binding material.

844
00:43:56,760 --> 00:43:59,060
Without the tenacity of the wheat straw,

845
00:43:59,160 --> 00:44:02,000
the bricks would crumble.

846
00:44:02,100 --> 00:44:05,100
Whether you turn to wheat to build your home, quench your

847
00:44:05,200 --> 00:44:09,420
thirst, or feed your family, the fate of the crop ultimately

848
00:44:09,520 --> 00:44:13,420
depends on the brigade of more than 700 custom harvesters,

849
00:44:13,520 --> 00:44:17,020
making the four-month long harvest run from Texas to Canada.

850
00:44:17,021 --> 00:44:20,420
Canada.

851
00:44:20,520 --> 00:44:24,260
It's a responsibility the custom cutter Ron Meisner has gladly

852
00:44:24,360 --> 00:44:27,660
accepted for 37 years.

853
00:44:27,760 --> 00:44:30,260
It's a very, very tough business.

854
00:44:30,360 --> 00:44:31,660
I've seen a lot of people come.

855
00:44:31,760 --> 00:44:34,240
I've seen a lot of people go.

856
00:44:34,340 --> 00:44:36,880
I will be doing this till the day I die,

857
00:44:36,980 --> 00:44:39,380
and it's going to be hard for them to plant me in the cemetery

858
00:44:39,480 --> 00:44:43,420
because I'm going down in a 9600 John Deere combine.

859
00:44:43,520 --> 00:44:45,420
And the Meisner crew knows it's them

860
00:44:45,421 --> 00:44:47,760
versus weather and time.

861
00:44:47,860 --> 00:44:50,760
At stake is a harvest of gold.

